 "findings": [
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/references/full/verify.md",
   "fp": "6362d0bcb1a3",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 109,
   "message": "Two readers interpret `workflow.security_review` differently. `specclaw-pr` uses `sec_bool`, which treats `no`, `off`, `False` and a quoted `\"false\"` as off and refuses unknown values. The plan step (3c), the build step (2c) and verify Step 3.52 tell the agent to skip only when the value is \"explicitly `false`\", so any other spelling reads as on. Fix: have the three docs name the same spellings as `sec_bool` (true/yes/on, false/no/off, any case or quoting; anything else is a config error), or have them call one shared reader.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B4",
   "scenario": "config `security_review: off` \u2192 `/specclaw:pr` skips the security gate entirely, while plan, build and verify still spawn infosec-reviewer and write reports the PR never checks.",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/bin/specclaw-pr",
   "fp": "a9c651b978e9",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 885,
   "message": "The waiver hint prints `- Waiver: <path> | <rule> | accepted risk|false alarm | <reason>`. Because `|` is the field separator, a literal copy parses as kind `accepted risk` with reason `false alarm | <reason>`. Write the choice outside the field syntax, for example `<accepted risk or false alarm>`.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B10",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/tests/evals/infosec/dotnet-forwarded-rate-limit/fixed.cs",
   "fp": "4e8985d5bfce",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 10,
   "message": "The 'fixed' samples for dotnet-forwarded-rate-limit and python-forwarded-rate-limit key the login limit on the client IP only, and the dotnet one hard-codes `PermitLimit = 10`. The shipped presets' **RateLimit** rule requires keying on the account as well as the client, and dotnet also requires the limit to be required config. A reviewer that correctly flags this as BLOCK is scored as a false alarm, which skews the false-alarm rate. Add the account to the partition key, and read the limit from required config.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B2",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  },
  {
   "file": "plugins/specclaw/bin/specclaw-security-gate",
   "fp": "010bed90de0a",
   "in_delta": true,
   "line": 85,
   "message": "A verdict value with a leading emoji or other decoration (for example `**Verdict:** \ud83d\udd34 CHANGES_REQUESTED`) does not match VERDICT_WORDS. It becomes '?', and the gate exits 3 with 'security review did not run'. The outcome still fails closed, but the message is wrong for a review that ran and requested changes. Strip leading non-word characters before matching, or report it as 'verdict unreadable' rather than 'did not run'.",
   "original_severity": "minor",
   "pass": "review",
   "pre_existing": false,
   "rule": "B3",
   "scenario": "",
   "severity": "minor"
  }
 ],
